  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Physical Description
    
    A pressure-forged trident of considerable mass. Three prongs carved from whale-bone, darkened by pressure-magic to a charred appearance. Deep-sea coral binds the shaft in a grip permanently stained — accounts consistently describe penguin blood that resists salt-water cleaning, though whether this is a property of the coral or the magic is unattested. The central prong bears a hairline fracture, discussed below, which weeps a substance resembling water but too viscous for it. The weapon is associated with a resonant hum; after bones from one of its shore-raids were incorporated into the Threk Boneday ritual, this sound reportedly persisted without the stimulus of bloodshed, as though something in the bone-work had activated a frequency the trident could not stop answering.
    
    ## Provenance and Use
    
    Grungruk's Claw was carried for eight seasons by Zund Gnasharkin, an orca war-leader during the Orca Incursion. The defining fracture appeared when Zund drove the trident upward through the crystalline vault during the Collapse, bringing down half the cavern on both armies. The crack has spread since — slowly, by all accounts, though no one has measured it systematically.
    
    Zund organized the Shelf Crushing Offense against the Nightfall Shelf and died in its execution, pinned beneath collapsing ice. Four penguins recovered the trident from his grip. Two did not survive the week. The summary records three orca who subsequently attempted to claim the weapon: one returned pressure-sick with shattered echolocation and died within a season; the other two vanished entirely.
    
    ## Current Disposition
    
    The trident came to rest in corrupted ice within the Thewsed Slew, a Nightshelf forge-temple. Multiple accounts describe autonomous killings in the surrounding tunnels — Zund Gnasharkin's kill-marks carved into the ice as though his hand still guided the work. Six moon-cycles after the Shelf Crushing Offense, Grul Grulrend, Zund's pod-brother, tracked the weapon to this location. The chronicle account states the corrupted ice transmitted impressions of Zund's final moments. Grul Grulrend lifted the trident, felt the fracture widen, and set it back.
    
    The weapon remains entombed in memory-ice at depth. It has not been recovered. The fracture, presumably, continues to spread, though no one has returned to verify this — or if they have, they did not report back.
